What to Submit
Upload one Week 5 package to Canvas containing:

Progress Report 2 using the standard four-question structure;
the Search Record;
your refined research question from the Week 5 rebuild worksheet; and
an annotated core-source table or Zotero export showing at least 15 purposefully selected sources and five substantive annotations.

What Progress Report 2 Must Cover
Build the report around the standard four questions, but make these parts visible:

which next actions from your Week 3 meeting you completed, and which slipped;
what changed when you rebuilt the question in Week 5, and what forced the change;
the published result you are bringing to Week 6, and why it matters to your project; and
the questions or design uncertainties that remain open.

What the Search Record Must Cover
Record:

the research question or concepts that bounded the search;
each database, review article, or other starting point used;
the major terms, filters, and backward or forward citation chains tried;
at least one search that failed or was too broad, and what you changed;
why the current core sources were included;
sources excluded after inspection and the reason, when relevant; and
unresolved evidence or a discipline-specific database still to search.

The record must let a reader reconstruct the major search decisions and understand why the retained sources fit the question.

Satisfactory Standard
The milestone is satisfactory when:

all four report questions are answered specifically rather than generally;
the Search Record makes the search path reconstructable;
the source set is purposeful rather than just numerous; and
the package shows how the question, the search decisions, and the retained sources fit together.

Monday: Match the Question to the Approach
Download the printable question sort (PDF)
Twelve unlabeled research questions, written for this exercise rather than taken from anyone’s thesis. Working in pairs, you will:

decide what form the answer to each question would have to take;
name the approach each question requires, and which ones could go either way;
record the two questions you disagreed about most, and why;
rewrite one question so it requires the other approach; and
name one conclusion the rewritten version supports that the original did not.

The last two steps are the ones that teach. A question is not quantitative or qualitative because of its topic, but because of what would count as an answer.

Submission Checklist
Upload one Week 10 package containing:

a title;
an introduction with the research question stated directly;
a synthesized literature review;
a methods section; and
Zotero-generated references.

Also include:

the completed Early Advisor Design Check worksheet and a dated, redacted record of the request or exchange, plus a short summary of the design decision or unresolved question it produced; and
the UHP Form Readiness Check, if you are a spring or summer graduate who must file the separate UHP Thesis Proposal Form by December 1.

UHP Form Readiness Check
Students graduating in spring or summer also submit:

the working title;
the brief prospectus;
confirmed committee chair;
committee-composition status; and
any missing approval with the date it will be resolved.

Satisfactory Standard
This part is satisfactory when:

all four funnel steps are attempted;
the final claim could be checked and could turn out to be wrong;
the diagnostic marks the claim, evidence, and reasoning or identifies where one of those parts is absent;
the revision makes at least one specific improvement in precision, clarity, or qualification; and
the explanation names the exact change that helped.

This is in-class draft work. It does not need to produce your final thesis question or a polished paragraph.
If an absence or access need makes the paper hand-in unworkable, contact the instructor for an arranged digital alternative.

What the Document Contains
The complete document includes the sections required by your program. The working course structure is:

title and project identification;
introduction and research question;
literature review;
proposed methods;
expected results and alternative outcomes, or actual results and discussion for Fall Graduation students;
discussion of significance and limitations;
figures or tables when appropriate; and
references.

Program-specific requirements take precedence over this working list. This is also not the UHP Thesis Proposal Form, which is a separate administrative filing asking only for a title, brief prospectus, and committee chair.

What You Will Complete

a requirements audit separating confirmed UHP rules, confirmed program rules, and unconfirmed questions;
a short fit analysis for three possible faculty advisors;
one complete first-contact message of no more than 200 words; and
a peer test asking whether the message could have been sent unchanged to any faculty member in the college.

The Advisor FAQ explains the course commitment and includes an example message. The example is a structure, not text to copy unchanged.

Satisfactory Standard
The worksheet is satisfactory when it:

records the official sources searched and distinguishes confirmed rules from open questions;
names the person or office that can resolve each important open question;
identifies three plausible faculty candidates and connects each person to a specific work item;
includes one complete message with who you are, why this person fits, and a concrete request; and
records specific peer feedback and one revision made in response.

Complete the other two messages by Friday, September 18. Send all three no later than Monday, September 21, then keep dated copies for Milestone 4.






NoteFall Graduation Thesis Completion Track



Use the track section of the worksheet to audit thesis-completion requirements, name the largest unresolved requirement, identify the Primary Advisor and/or Proposal Committee member who can resolve it, and draft a message requesting a specific decision or reserving the professional-review window.

What Advisor-ready Means
A draft is advisor-ready when:

every required section exists in some form, with no placeholder headings;
the research question appears in the introduction, stated directly;
the literature review is organized around themes and supports the opening your question addresses;
the methods would let a reader judge whether the question can be answered;
appropriate figures, tables, and captions are present;
each retained visual passes the Visual Audit and Caption Clinic stand-alone test;
the abstract is drafted; and
references are complete and Zotero-generated.

What to Submit
Upload:

the Advisor-ready Full Thesis Proposal or, on the Fall Graduation track, the Advisor-ready Full Thesis Draft;
a completed one-page Whole-Document Revision Memo naming the three most important changes made after peer whole-document review, the evidence in the draft that prompted each change, and any issue that still requires expert judgment;
the materials required for the release screen; and
confirmation that the released version was sent to the Primary Advisor and/or Proposal Committee by the end of Wednesday, November 25.

Whole-Document Review
Before the release screen, exchange the complete draft with a peer and trace:

the research question and why it matters;
what the literature establishes, complicates, or leaves unresolved;
how the proposed method answers that exact question;
what outcomes would be distinguishable using that method; and
what limits, feasibility constraints, or program requirements remain.

Method and Feasibility Blueprint
Week 6, due Wed, Oct 14, on paper. Use the printable form for your track, or type your own using its headings. A concise design memo that carries the methods and evidence work of Weeks 5 and 6 into your proposal. Use headings. A table is fine. Length is not the target; reviewable decisions are.
Include:

your current research question and the quantitative, qualitative, mixed, or discipline-specific design it requires;
the unit, population, system, cases, or materials you will study;
the evidence or data you need and how you expect to obtain it;
the collection, measurement, comparison, and analysis steps you can name at this stage;
feasibility constraints, including access, time, equipment, sample or case availability, permissions, safety, cost, and ethics or human-subjects review when applicable;
one important risk and a feasible alternative if the preferred design does not work;
one published claim whose wording you would narrow to match its evidence; and
one specific design question for your Primary Advisor.

Mark an item “not applicable” and explain why when it genuinely does not fit your field. Do not invent details to fill a box.
The blueprint is satisfactory when a reader can trace the question to the proposed evidence and analysis, identify the largest feasibility constraint, and see what still requires advisor or specialist judgment. The claim example must show both the original wording and a narrowed version you would accept.






NoteFall Graduation Track



Apply the same structure to completed thesis work. Submit a Thesis Method, Analysis, and Feasibility Audit naming the design actually used, data or evidence obtained, analysis complete and outstanding, ethics or permissions status when applicable, the strongest supported claim, at least one claim needing qualification, the largest completion risk, and the dated response to that risk.

What to Bring
Bring:

your current literature-synthesis draft;
one printed copy for your partner;
the printed draft in 12-point Times New Roman, double spaced, with continuous line numbers; and
whatever notes you need to explain your question and source-set boundary.

Required Sections

Foundations, Weeks 1-4

Week 1 paper-dissection and claims-and-evidence handout;
Interest, Motivation, and Direction Freewrite;
writing diagnostic and revision;
program-requirements audit;
reproducible search record and source-selection notes; and
Progress Report 1.



Evidence and Methods, Weeks 5-8

research-question revisions;
Method and Feasibility Blueprint;
statistical-claim inspection;
synthesis matrix or equivalent organizing tool;
Literature Synthesis Draft; and
Progress Reports 2 and 3.



Proposal Development, Weeks 9-12

proposal map;
peer review given and received;
writer responses to both peer reviews;
introduction and methods drafts;
Early Advisor Design Check or Early Professional Review;
expected-results alternatives;
figure, table, or abstract revisions; and
Professional Review Plan.



Revision and Completion, Weeks 13-15

instructor-conversation and release notes;
Primary Advisor and/or Proposal Committee feedback;
prioritized revision plan;
response memo; and
final reflection.

Students in the Fall Graduation Thesis Completion Track should substitute actual-results and thesis-completion artifacts for proposal-only artifacts. Include the thesis-status audit, results-and-claims audit, advisor-ready thesis draft, and UHP completion preparation.


Mentor Interaction Log
Record meetings, messages, decisions, and feedback from your thesis advisor. Do not include private information that is unrelated to the project.
A dated entry and your own summary of the project decision are sufficient. Redact personal contact information and unrelated material. Raw email threads, calendar records, and unredacted reviewer comments are optional and are not expected.

Final Reflection
Address:

how your research question changed;
how searching and reading changed your understanding of the field;
what you learned about technical writing and revision;
how feedback affected the proposal;
which professional communication skills you strengthened; and
what you will do next with your advisor.

Learning Objectives
By the end of the course, students should be able to:

identify and articulate a research domain and thesis topic;
navigate library databases and conduct systematic literature searches;
write technical prose using appropriate disciplinary conventions;
distinguish quantitative and qualitative methodologies and select an approach appropriate to a research question;
evaluate statistical claims, study designs, and evidence quality;
construct or revise the major sections of a thesis proposal or thesis;
provide constructive, specific peer feedback;
communicate professionally with mentors, committee members, and other academic professionals; and
complete an honors thesis proposal or thesis draft that meets the applicable track and program requirements.

How to Succeed in This Course
Students who succeed in this course typically:

attend consistently and come prepared;
start writing early and revise often;
seek clarification early when concepts are unclear;
treat the course as both a technical and a creative endeavor; and
contact their thesis advisor early and communicate regularly.

If you find yourself struggling, please reach out during office hours or by appointment, talk to classmates, since peer support is part of the course design, and ask for extensions early. Life happens.

Technology You Will Need
No statistical software is required in this course. You will need:

a laptop or desktop computer for word processing and database access;
a stable internet connection, for the course site, Canvas, and library databases;
Canvas, for grades, private feedback, protected student records, and the assignments designated for online upload;
Zotero or a similar reference manager. Free and open source; installation is covered in Week 4; and
University library databases, including Web of Science, PubMed, CABI, and discipline-specific collections through UMN Libraries.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

judge your own document against a readiness standard rather than against how much work you put into it;
explain how your research question, evidence base, and writing changed across the term;
name the decisions that still require advisor or committee review; and
state the first concrete action you will take after the course.

Wednesday: Course Close

Process Portfolio
The portfolio should document:

how your research question changed;
how searching and reading changed your understanding of the field;
what you learned about technical writing and revision;
how peer, instructor, and professional-review feedback affected the document;
which professional communication skills you strengthened; and
the next actions you will take with your advisor.



Transition to Thesis Research
Your proposal or thesis is a working plan, not a promise that the project will unfold without change. Record the decisions that still require advisor or committee review, the evidence still needed, and the first concrete action after the course.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish quantitative and qualitative research by what each one can establish, rather than by topic;
decide which approach your question requires, and say why;
refine a research question using PICO, meaning population, intervention, comparison, and outcome, or a structure better matched to the question you actually have;
judge whether a finding changes what you may conclude, or only what you know; and
state the significance of your question against the “so what?” test.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain the purpose of each major proposal section;
trace a research question through the introduction, literature review, methods, and expected results;
distinguish structural feedback from sentence-level editing;
provide specific, evidence-based peer feedback; and
identify the highest-priority revision in a draft.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

convert professional feedback into a prioritized revision plan;
distinguish structural revision from sentence-level editing;
explain why you accepted, modified, or declined major feedback;
identify the remaining work required before the applicable final deadline; and
communicate professionally with the Primary Advisor and/or Proposal Committee about unresolved decisions.

Wednesday: Feedback Triage and Revision Workshop
Bring the professional feedback and the current document. After a short model, sort comments into four categories:

research question or scope;
evidence and literature structure;
method, analysis, and feasibility; and
clarity, organization, and presentation.

For each consequential comment, record:

the action you will take;
why you will accept, modify, or decline the recommendation;
the section affected;
whether another decision from the Primary Advisor or Proposal Committee is required; and
the date the revision will be complete.

Most of Wednesday is revision time. Start with changes that affect the logic of the whole document before polishing sentences.

Triage Order
Work in this order:

question, scope, or claim changes that alter the logic of the whole document;
evidence, literature, or method revisions that change what the document can support;
section-level reorganization; and
sentence-level editing and formatting.

If two reviewers disagree, do not treat that as a mistake to erase. Treat it as a decision to justify. The response memo is where you show what each reader was optimizing for and what you will do next.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish a research claim from the evidence offered for it, and identify the population, comparison, outcome, and design standing behind it;
state what a p-value does and does not measure, and separate statistical significance from practical importance;
distinguish association from causation, and judge whether a paper’s own wording stays inside what its design supports;
explain why selective analysis and reporting inflate the published record, and name what a reader can check;
read a figure or table for what it hides as well as what it shows; and
say why a mixed-methods design would or would not serve your question.

Wednesday: Evidence Detectives
Bring two papers from your own field and audit them. You are not recomputing anything. You are working out what the authors are entitled to say.

What to Bring
Two papers, printed, that meet all of these:

primary research, not a review or a commentary;
reports numbers of some kind, whether means, counts, frequencies, scores, or participants;
includes at least one figure or table of results; and
close enough to your own question that you would consider citing it.

The numbers requirement is not a quantitative-only requirement. An interview study reports how many people were approached, how many participated, and how many raised a given theme. A sensory panel reports scores. Those are the numbers this audit is about.
Print them. You will be marking up displays and circling numbers, and the audit does not work if you are reading over someone’s shoulder at a laptop.


The Audit
Three questions do most of the work, and all three are answerable without software:

How many? Find the number, and find where it shrank. Studies report how many were enrolled, approached, or planted, then analyze a smaller number. The gap is where problems hide. Ask whether that final number supports a claim about a population, or only about the units actually measured. This question does not change between designs. A theme reported by three of 22 interviewees and written up as “participants described” has the same problem as a yield mean with four surviving plots.
How much? Find the size of the effect and its units, separately from any p-value. Significance answers “is it real?” Effect size answers “does it matter?” You need both. Where the work is not reporting effects, ask the parallel question: how much of the evidence is this claim resting on?
What is the display not showing? A bar chart of means hides the spread. A table of percentages hides its denominator. A table of means hides the variation just as completely as the bar chart does. Look for whether you can see the variation, the individual observations, or the uncertainty. If you cannot, ask why not.

Then one check carried over from Monday: what does this paper let you find out about how its analysis was chosen? Look for whether the analysis plan was specified in advance, whether the primary outcome named in the methods is the one featured in the abstract, and how many outcomes or comparisons were examined against how many were reported. Often you will not be able to tell. Write that down. “Cannot determine” is the finding, and it is the reason principle four asks for full reporting.


Group Report
Complete a Claim Worksheet for each paper, then report out on one of them:

the strongest conclusion the evidence supports;
one conclusion the evidence does not support;
how many units were actually analyzed, and what that limits the claim to;
whether the effect or pattern is large enough to matter in that research context;
what the figures and tables conceal about variation;
whether the paper’s own wording is associational or causal, and whether the design supports the version it uses;
what the paper lets you check about how its analysis was chosen, and what it does not;
one design or measurement limitation;
one question you would ask the authors; and
whether and how you could use the result in a proposal.

The worksheet allows “not reported” and “cannot determine.” Use them. A question the paper does not let you answer is a finding about the paper, not a blank you failed to fill.
Across the room, expect at least one paper where the statistics are sound and the sentence describing them is not. That gap, between what was found and what was said, is the thing you are training to see. Expect at least one paper that says exactly what its evidence supports, too. Recognizing that is the same skill running in the other direction, and it is the reason this course is not teaching you to distrust everything you read.

Part 1: Introductions
You will receive a table nameplate when you arrive. Put the name you want me and your classmates to use on the front. You may add pronouns if you want to.
The back is for me rather than for public sharing. It asks for:

your full name as it appears in Canvas;
how to pronounce your name;
where you are from or consider home;
your degree path or program;
your year in school;
whether you are graduating in Fall 2026;
two favorite colors; and
one unusual, interesting, or “weird” fact you would like me to know.

You may skip any personal prompt. These cards stay with me and are not graded.
For the brief spoken introduction, share your preferred name, program or degree path, and one research interest, motivating problem, or optional unusual fact. The introductions take time, and that time matters. This course depends on conversation, individual meetings, and feedback across disciplines. We begin by learning who is in the room.

Part 4: Your Work for the Rest of the Week

Interest, Motivation, and Direction Freewrite
Do not search for three faculty members yet. First, give yourself something worth searching from.
Write informally about:

the problems, systems, questions, or experiences that hold your attention;
why those interests matter to you;
what motivates you to investigate or work on them;
the kinds of activities or work you enjoy doing;
a role, job, profession, or career that might involve that kind of work;
what you already know and what you would need to explore; and
one uncertainty you want help thinking through.

This is exploratory writing. You do not need a settled thesis topic, a research question, or polished prose. Spend enough time to produce material you can use in Monday’s topic-funnel exercise.
Use the accessible assignment sheet or download the printable PDF. Bring one printed copy to class on Monday, September 14. Use Times New Roman, 12 point, double spacing, one-inch margins, continuous line numbers, and single-sided printing. You will use the freewrite in the topic-funnel activity and hand it in at the end of class. The result will appear in Canvas, but there is no routine Canvas upload. This is the second participation component of Milestone 1. It is evaluated against the exploratory standard on the assignment sheet, not for technical polish.


Fall Graduation Thesis Completion Track
Use the same prompts through the thesis you are already completing. Write about why the project mattered, how it connects to work or a future direction, what still motivates you, what you have learned about the work you want to do, and the most important uncertainty or threat to completion. We will address the formal thesis-status and deadline audit in the planning sequence that follows.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

state a research domain and defend why it is viable for a thesis;
name the specific connection between your interests and each faculty member you have contacted;
articulate an emerging research question, however rough, out loud and under questioning; and
name the obstacle currently slowing your project and what you have already tried.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish summary from synthesis, and tell which one a paragraph of your own draft is doing;
organize sources by question, population, method, finding, or disagreement, using the same slots you used on your own question in Week 5;
state a pattern that holds across several sources, and a disagreement that does not resolve;
explain how the literature narrows your question and warrants your objectives; and
name a defensible gap bounded by your search, without claiming that no prior work exists; and
judge whether you have read enough by sketching the tables and figures your own study would produce, ranking your treatments or cases, and defending that order from the literature.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

design a figure with labeled axes, units, a legend, and no decoration that carries no information;
build a table whose headers, alignment, and ordering are readable linearly;
write a caption self-contained enough that the figure survives being read on its own;
distinguish a table caption from a figure legend and write each one using the information a reader needs; and
draft a 250 to 300 word abstract covering context, gap, objective, method, and expected contribution.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain your document’s central argument without reading from it;
defend your design choices, assumptions, and limitations under questioning;
name the revisions required while professional review is underway; and
describe what changed in your question, evidence, and writing across the term.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

assemble the sections written to date into one coherent draft;
structure that draft as a unified argument rather than a set of disconnected sections;
deliver a 30-second pitch for your research that a listener can repeat back; and
identify the missing evidence, transitions, and design details in a complete draft.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

state your refined research question using Population, Intervention, Comparison, and Outcome (PICO) or a disciplinary equivalent;
name the paradigm your question requires and say why;
describe the themes emerging from your literature and where they conflict;
identify the claim or source in your evidence base that needs the most scrutiny; and
name what stands between you and a complete proposal or thesis.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish informal explanation from technical academic prose;
revise sentences for precision, clarity, and appropriate qualification;
narrow a research domain to a claim that could turn out to be wrong;
write a first-contact message a specific faculty member can act on;
identify the required parts of an honors thesis proposal;
locate the requirements that apply to your program; and
connect those requirements to a working proposal timeline.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

translate a research question into searchable concept groups;
choose a database or disciplinary collection based on its coverage;
revise a query by recording what the results reveal;
distinguish a review, a primary study, and gray literature by the work each source does;
organize sources in Zotero with verified metadata, consistent tags, and usable notes; and
trace a useful source backward and forward through its citation network.

First Claims-and-Evidence Ledger Entry
Use the work above to create a compact entry you can revisit later. Record:

the claim;
the evidence and its location;
why the evidence is relevant to this claim;
a limit or open question; and
the current status: tentative, needs closer reading, or revised.

The Reading Protocol provides the longer procedure we will reuse later in the semester.

Search Record Attached to Progress Report 2
Progress Report 2 includes a compact search record. This is how you show that the source set was built through decisions rather than accumulated by chance. Record:

the research question or concepts that bounded the search;
each database, review article, or other starting point used;
the major terms, filters, and backward or forward citation chains tried;
at least one search that failed or was too broad, and what you changed;
why the current core sources were included;
sources excluded after inspection and the reason, when relevant; and
unresolved evidence or a discipline-specific database still to search.

Attach the annotated core-source table or Zotero export showing at least 15 purposefully selected sources and five substantive annotations. A source count alone is not satisfactory. The record must let a reader reconstruct the major search decisions and understand why the retained sources fit the question.
Any milestone rated not yet satisfactory may be revised and resubmitted once within one week. See the resubmission policy.

What to Write
Write informally about:

problems, systems, questions, or experiences that hold your attention;
why those interests matter to you;
what motivates you to investigate or work on them;
the kinds of activities or work you enjoy doing;
a role, job, profession, or career that might involve that kind of work;
what you already know and what you would need to explore; and
one uncertainty you want help thinking through.

This is exploratory writing. It does not need a research question, faculty names, citations, or polished prose.

Peer Review 2
Exchange your methods section and expected-results draft with a partner and use the printable Peer Review 2 worksheet to test:

the argument chain from research question through outcome logic;
whether the unit, population, system, cases, or materials match the question;
one missing design or analysis detail that would keep a reader from judging feasibility or answerability;
the applicable constraints, including access, time, permissions, ethics, and alternatives; and
whether plausible outcomes are distinguishable from each other, or, for a completed thesis, whether the claims stay within the actual results.

Then recommend the single highest-priority revision and explain why it comes before sentence-level editing. Use the Week 6: Method and Feasibility Blueprint as a reference when checking whether the method is inspectable.

What to Bring
Bring:

the completed Conversation Brief and Review Confirmation worksheet;
your released full proposal or thesis, sent for professional review by November 25;
your process portfolio, in progress; and
your current list of unresolved evidence, method, and feasibility questions.

Proposal Development Track
Download the printable Progress Report 1 form (PDF)
Your report should make these parts of your current project visible:

the domain and rough research question produced by your Freewrite and topic funnel;
the three faculty members you contacted, any replies, and what you learned from preparing or sending the messages;
one program requirement or timeline question that remains unresolved;
the obstacle currently slowing the project and what you have already tried; and
one dated next action.

Place those details under the four standard questions. Do not add a fifth section or turn the report into an essay.

Fall Graduation Thesis Completion Track
Download the printable Thesis Progress Report 1 form (PDF)
Your report should make these parts of your current thesis visible:

work completed since the Week 2 Thesis Completion Plan;
the largest obstacle to completing the thesis this term and what you have already tried;
the status of the Primary Advisor and Proposal Committee;
the remaining analysis, writing, professional review, and University Honors Program completion actions; and
the questions that must be resolved in the meeting.

Use specific dates for the next period. A plan such as “finish the analysis” is not specific enough to test at the next checkpoint.

Satisfactory Standard
The report is satisfactory when it:

answers all four standard questions;
names the project or thesis status specifically enough for another person to act on it;
distinguishes an obstacle from the steps already taken to address it;
gives at least one dated next action; and
arrives before the individual meeting and is handed in afterward.

A report may say that you are stuck. Satisfactory describes the quality and honesty of the report, not whether the project is proceeding without trouble.
If an access need makes the paper hand-in unworkable, contact the instructor for an arranged digital alternative.

Final Readiness Check
Before submitting, confirm:

the research question is specific and visible;
the literature review synthesizes sources around the question;
claims are supported and appropriately qualified;
the proposed method or completed work addresses the question;
limitations and feasibility constraints are acknowledged;
figures and tables can be understood from their captions;
references are complete and consistent; and
program-specific requirements have been checked.

Use the full standard on The Document.

Proposal Development Track
Download the printable Method and Feasibility Blueprint (PDF)
Eight sections:

the research question, the design it requires, and what a single strand cannot deliver for it;
the unit, population, system, cases, or materials you will study;
the evidence or data you need and how you expect to obtain it;
the collection, measurement, comparison, and analysis steps you can name now;
feasibility constraints, including ethics, permissions, and human-subjects review where they apply, and which constraint is largest;
one important risk and a feasible alternative;
one published claim narrowed to what its evidence supports; and
one specific design question for your Primary Advisor.


Satisfactory
A reader can trace the question to the proposed evidence and analysis, identify the largest feasibility constraint, and see what still requires advisor or specialist judgment. The claim example must show both the original wording and the narrowed version you would accept. Section one must name what one strand cannot deliver on its own; if you mark the design mixed, it must also name the connecting logic and the point where the two strands meet.
Section four is the one students most often skip past. Naming what you have not yet decided is worth more than a confident guess, because an unresolved decision your advisor can see is a decision they can help with.

Fall Graduation Thesis Completion Track
Download the printable Thesis Method, Analysis, and Feasibility Audit (PDF)
The same structure, aimed at work already done:

the design actually used, where it departed from the plan, and, where more than one strand is involved, the point at which they meet;
the data or evidence obtained;
analysis complete and outstanding;
ethics or permissions status, when applicable;
the strongest claim your completed work supports;
at least one claim needing qualification, in both its current and narrowed wording;
the largest completion risk and a dated response to it; and
one specific question for your Primary Advisor or Proposal Committee.


Satisfactory
A reader can see what the completed work supports, what it does not, the largest completion risk, and a dated response to that risk. Where the work uses more than one strand, the audit must say what each one carries and where they meet.

Search Workbook
Download the printable Week 4 Search Workbook (PDF)
Use the workbook in both meetings to record:

your current question or focused problem;
two to four searchable concept groups and their alternate terms;
the database, date, exact query, filters, and result count for each attempt;
what changed between attempts and why;
the role and fit decision for two sources;
one pearl, five backward sources, and five forward sources;
Zotero discovery-path tags and metadata corrections; and
structured notes for one pearl, one backward source, and one forward source.

