Fall Graduation Milestone
Download the printable Literature and Reference Audit (PDF)
Hand in the audit on Wednesday, September 30. Inspect the current thesis and reference library by section. Record specific:

claims that need stronger or additional support;
sources that are missing or not yet retrieved;
incomplete or incorrect citation metadata;
mismatches between in-text citations and the reference list; and
dated actions needed before the full thesis review.

The audit is satisfactory when it identifies concrete gaps and the sections they affect. “The references need work” is not an audit.

What the Draft Must Do
Satisfactory means the draft:

explains the source-set boundary, including databases or starting sources, major terms or citation chains, inclusion reasoning, and unresolved evidence;
uses traceable citations to sources you have read closely enough to describe their method, finding, and limitation;
organizes sources around themes rather than one source per paragraph;
states at least one pattern that holds across multiple sources;
names a disagreement or limitation and explains why it matters for your question; and
identifies a gap without claiming that no prior work exists.

Before You Upload
Use the Week 8 independent sequence on the module page to finish the draft:

finish the relevant synthesis-matrix rows;
draft at least one synthesis paragraph;
self-diagnose where the paragraph summarizes instead of synthesizes;
revise the paragraph; and
record one question you want answered in the feedback.

Proposal Development Track
Download the printable Progress Report 3 form (PDF)
Your report should make these parts of your current project visible:

Primary Advisor status, and the preliminary committee plan if you have started one;
your refined research question, in PICO or the slot structure your question needs;
where the literature stands: what you have read closely, the themes emerging, and where those themes conflict;
the claim or source in your evidence base that most needs scrutiny;
the Blueprint design decision that most needs advisor review; and
the Early Advisor Design Check plan: who reviews it, what you will send, and by what date.

Place those details under the four standard questions. Do not add a fifth section or turn the report into an essay.
The Early Advisor Design Check is due in Week 10, and the materials go to your advisor before the Monday, November 9 session. Naming the plan here is what makes that deadline reachable. See Written Assignments for what the check requires.

Satisfactory Standard
The report is satisfactory when it:

answers all four standard questions;
names the project or thesis status specifically enough for another person to act on it;
distinguishes an obstacle from the steps already taken to address it;
gives at least one dated next action; and
arrives before the individual meeting and is handed in afterward.

For the Fall Graduation track, the Professional Review Plan is satisfactory when every person named on it has agreed to the review window. A plan naming reviewers who have not been asked is not yet satisfactory.
A report may say that you are stuck. Satisfactory describes the quality and honesty of the report, not whether the project is proceeding without trouble.
If an access need makes the paper hand-in unworkable, contact the instructor for an arranged digital alternative.

What to Submit
Upload the Primary Advisor and/or Proposal Committee Review and Response package containing:

evidence that the document was sent for professional review;
reviewer comments, an annotated draft, or meeting notes;
a response memo explaining which major recommendations you will accept, modify, or decline; and
a prioritized revision plan for the applicable final deadline: December 14 on the Fall Graduation track or December 16 on the Proposal Development track.

How to Triage the Feedback
Sort consequential comments into four categories:

research question or scope;
evidence and literature structure;
method, analysis, and feasibility; and
clarity, organization, and presentation.

For each consequential comment, record:

the action you will take;
why you will accept, modify, or decline it;
the section affected;
whether another decision from the Primary Advisor or Proposal Committee is required; and
the date the revision will be complete.
